1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,748
Section five: staging models.

2
00:00:03,028 --> 00:00:05,865
They take a source and make it clean and consistent.

3
00:00:06,145 --> 00:00:11,862
We write the first one by hand, so you see exactly what a model is, and the agent writes the big one.

4
00:00:12,962 --> 00:00:18,061
If you remember one thing: a dbt model is just a select statement in a file.

5
00:00:18,341 --> 00:00:19,877
You never write create table.

6
00:00:20,157 --> 00:00:25,448
The file name becomes the model name, and dbt adds the right create statement for you.

7
00:00:26,548 --> 00:00:28,745
The SQL models page, version one.

8
00:00:29,025 --> 00:00:31,350
A model is one select in a SQL file.

9
00:00:31,630 --> 00:00:34,275
The file name is the name of the view or table.

10
00:00:34,556 --> 00:00:38,652
To use another model, you use ref, and dbt works out the order.

11
00:00:38,932 --> 00:00:40,275
The default is a view.

12
00:00:41,376 --> 00:00:44,810
On the left, what we write: a select, with ref to the zone seed.

13
00:00:45,090 --> 00:00:51,917
On the right, what dbt sends to the database: a create view, with ref replaced by the real table name.

14
00:00:52,197 --> 00:00:55,781
The Compiled SQL tab in Rosetta shows this for any model.

15
00:00:56,881 --> 00:00:59,228
Every ref is an arrow in the lineage graph.

16
00:00:59,508 --> 00:01:03,092
The staging model uses the seed, so the seed is built first.

17
00:01:03,372 --> 00:01:10,561
Later, a dimension uses the staging model. dbt reads all these references and builds from left to right.

18
00:01:11,661 --> 00:01:13,389
The structure guide on staging.

19
00:01:13,669 --> 00:01:17,893
Name files S T G, then the source, two underscores, then the entity.

20
00:01:18,173 --> 00:01:21,373
Do renaming, type casting and simple calculations.

21
00:01:21,653 --> 00:01:23,317
Do not join or aggregate.

22
00:01:23,597 --> 00:01:25,154
Staging models are views.

23
00:01:26,254 --> 00:01:27,257
Our checklist.

24
00:01:27,537 --> 00:01:33,212
Rename to snake case, cast types, turn the yes or no flag into a boolean, and keep every row.

25
00:01:33,492 --> 00:01:36,585
No joins, no aggregations, and no filtering.

26
00:01:36,865 --> 00:01:38,721
The strange trips stay in staging.

27
00:01:39,001 --> 00:01:41,689
We flag them later, where the business rule belongs.

28
00:01:42,789 --> 00:01:44,794
Our first model, written by hand.

29
00:01:45,074 --> 00:01:47,655
A source step that selects from the seed with ref.

30
00:01:47,936 --> 00:01:50,837
A renamed step that gives every column a clean name.

31
00:01:51,117 --> 00:01:52,397
And a final select.

32
00:01:52,677 --> 00:01:54,874
The agent will follow the same pattern.

33
00:01:55,974 --> 00:02:03,697
We run it with the model button, open Preview to see the renamed zones, and open Compiled SQL to see ref replaced by the table.

34
00:02:03,977 --> 00:02:05,491
We never wrote view in the file.

35
00:02:05,771 --> 00:02:08,417
The folder setting from section two decided it.

36
00:02:09,517 --> 00:02:11,970
The trips model is bigger: twenty columns.

37
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:13,786
Timestamps end in at.

38
00:02:14,066 --> 00:02:15,623
Distance gets its unit.

39
00:02:15,903 --> 00:02:17,631
IDs become snake case.

40
00:02:17,911 --> 00:02:19,533
The flag becomes a boolean.

41
00:02:19,813 --> 00:02:21,285
Money becomes a decimal.

42
00:02:21,565 --> 00:02:23,634
And the file name is kept as source file.

43
00:02:23,914 --> 00:02:26,325
Repetitive work, perfect for the agent.

44
00:02:27,425 --> 00:02:36,769
In the demo, the agent creates the staging model for yellow trips, following our skill: snake case, the at suffix, a boolean, decimals, and source file.

45
00:02:37,049 --> 00:02:38,670
No filters, no joins.

46
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:40,977
Plus a YAML file with descriptions.

47
00:02:41,257 --> 00:02:42,942
Then it builds only this model.

48
00:02:44,042 --> 00:02:46,815
This is the shape we expect, and how we review it.

49
00:02:47,095 --> 00:02:49,165
It uses source, not a table name.

50
00:02:49,445 --> 00:02:51,066
Every money column is cast.

51
00:02:51,346 --> 00:02:55,207
And two things must not be there: a where clause, and a config block.

52
00:02:55,487 --> 00:02:59,733
If either appears, we ask the agent to remove it before keeping the file.

53
00:03:00,833 --> 00:03:05,953
Two new nodes in the lineage: our staging models, each fed by exactly one input.

54
00:03:06,233 --> 00:03:08,366
One to one, just as the guide says.
